% Rx_Cass_NS.in -- NS twin of Rx_Cass_FarField.in: the Primary is
% Element= NSReflector (single-member non-sequential group; geometry
% identical to the sequential twin Rx_Cass_NSseq.in, one Element= token
% apart).  Fixture for the dw_dsurf NSReflector eligibility gate
% (tDwDsurf) and the NS flow-of-light gate (tNsFlowOfLight).
% M2 carries its PHYSICAL aperture (ApVec 0.52): under flow-of-light NS
% candidate selection an UNBOUNDED next-element conic's back-crossing is
% a real first intersection and would shadow the train -- NS decks must
% bound competing surfaces (the iris-segment idiom).  Rays inside the
% secondary shadow (launch r < 0.52) legitimately strike the secondary's
% BACK first and die -- physics the sequential form cannot express, and
% why the twins are compared on an annular launch (Obscratn= 2.4,
% r_in 1.2).  The chief (r = 0, shadow class, obscured anyway) is
% geometrically dead on this twin: expect exactly one surface miss; do
% not use FEX / reset_xp machinery on this deck.
% History: pre-fix engines (before surfsub LNSFlowL, 2026-09-05) ran the
% NS candidate probe on the |L^2-mpr| proximity metric and lost EVERY
% ray inside launch r ~ 1.005 on this deck (razor-sharp wrong-turn
% boundary; macos/BRIEF_luis_round3.md).

